cifying and regulating origins of DNA replication in eukaryotic cells</article-title><source>Genes Dev</source><year>1996</year><volume>10</volume><fpage>2819</fpage><lpage>2830</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8918884</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ding</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>West</surname><given-names>RR</given-names></name><name><surname>Morphew</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Oakley</surname><given-names>BR</given-names></name><name><surname>McIntosh</surname><given-names>JR</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The  spindle pole body of <italic>Schizosaccharomyces pombe</italic>enters and leaves the nuclear envelope as the cell cycle proceeds</article-title><source>Mol Biol Cell</source><year>1997</year><volume>8</volume><fpage>1461</fpage><lpage>1479</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9285819</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Donaldson</surname><given-names>AD</given-names></name><name><surname>Kilmartin</surname><given-names>JV</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Spc42p: a phosphorylated component of the <italic>S. cerevisiae</italic>spindle pole body (SPB) with an essential function during SPB duplication</article-title><source>J Cell Biol</source><year>1996</year><volume>132</volume><fpage>887</fpage><lpage>901</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8603920</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gall</surname><given-names>JG</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Centriole replication</article-title><source>J Biophys Biochem Cytol</source><year>1961</year><volume>10</volume><fpage>163</fpage><lpage>193</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">13703108</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><ci